# Development

Initialize the module with `go mod init {{project_slug}}`, or with the intended
remote module path, replacing `{{project_slug}}` in the Go imports. The library
itself needs no modules. The API checker is a dev tool tracked in `tools.mod`,
a second module file, so it never reaches `go.mod`:

```sh
go mod init -modfile=tools.mod "$(go list -m)"
go get -modfile=tools.mod -tool golang.org/x/exp/cmd/apidiff
make install
```

Install golangci-lint using its official instructions and write its installed
version to `.golangci-lint-version`. Run `make help` for every target.

```sh
make fmt-check tidy-check vet lint test fuzz consumer coverage
go test -race -run 'TestParseErrors/1y' .
go test -run '^$' -fuzz '^FuzzParse$' -fuzztime 30s .
```

## Tests

- `duration_test.go` and `internal/lex/lex_test.go`: table tests for every
  path, each error checked with `errors.Is` and `errors.As`.
- `example_test.go`: the documentation examples. `go test` runs each one and
  compares its output with the `// Output:` comment.
- `fuzz_test.go`: properties that hold for any input. `make test` runs the
  seeds; `make fuzz` searches for FUZZTIME per target and saves a failing input
  under `testdata/fuzz/`, where it stays as a regression case.
- `tests/consumer`: calls the library as another module would. `make consumer`
  copies it into a temporary module that requires this one, so an unexported
  or `internal/` name fails to compile there.

## API baseline

`api/baseline.export` holds the exported API of the last release, written by
`make api-baseline` with apidiff. `make api-check` fails on any incompatible
change since then. Record the first baseline once the checks pass; after that it
moves only at a release (`docs/releasing.md`).
